          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            %em Bell’s inequality
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bell64"}> Bell 1964
            ) is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inequality"} inequality
            satisfied by the three pairwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+functions"} correlation functions
            between three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variables"} random variables
            defined on one and the same classical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+space"} probability space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Pitowsky89a"} Pitowsky 1989a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boole+--+The+Laws+of+Thought"} Boole (1854)
          %p
            The point of the argument by
            %a{:href => "#Bell64"} Bell 1964
            was to highlight that when taking these three random variables to be the results of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurements"} quantum measurements
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            along three pairwise non-orthogonal axes (as in the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stern-Gerlach+experiment"} Stern-Gerlach experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            predicts that this inequality is
            %em violated
            – implying that there is no single classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+space"} probability space
            (called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable+theory"} hidden variable
            in the context of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretations+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretations of quantum mechanics
            = succeed "-results" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variables"} random variables
          %p
            A number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            have sought to check Bell’s inequalities in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell+tests"}> Bell tests
            ”) and all claim to have verified that it is indeed violated in nature (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Aspect15"} Aspect 2015
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
          %p
            Bell’s inequality has been and is receiving an enormous amount of attention, first in discussions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretations+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretations of quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
          %h2#Statement Statement
          %h3#OriginalFormulation Original formulation
          %blockquote
            %p
              The following is fairly verbatim recap of the original argument in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Bell64"} Bell 1964
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#CompactReformulation"} below
          %p
            Let us denote the result
            %em A
            of a measurement that is determined by a unit vector,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(\vec{a},\lambda)=\pm 1
            where we further suppose that the outcome of the measurement is either +1 or -1. Likewise, we may do the same for the result
            %em B
            of a second measurement, i.e.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\vec{b},\lambda)
            %em B
            does not depend on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            and likewise
            %em A
            does not depend on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{b}
          %p
            Before proceeding, we should note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            here plays the role of a “hidden” parameter or variable. We say it is “hidden” because its precise nature is not known. However, it is still a very real parameter with a probability distribution
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(\lambda)
          #eq:AnExpectationValue.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(\vec{a},\vec{b})=\int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)A(\vec{a},\lambda)B(\vec{b},\lambda).
          %p
            Because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is a normalized probability distribution,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int d\lambda \rho(\lambda) = 1
          %p
            and because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(\vec{a},\lambda)=\pm 1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo ±
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\vec{b},\lambda)=\pm 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}=\vec{b}
            only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(\vec{a},\lambda)=\pm 1 = -B(\vec{a},\lambda)=\pm 1
            except at a set of points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            of zero probability. Thus we can write
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:AnExpectationValue"} (1)
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(\vec{a},\vec{b})=-\int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)A(\vec{a},\lambda)A(\vec{b},\lambda).
          %p
            If we introduce a third unit vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{c}
            we can find the difference between the correlation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            to the two other unit vectors,
          #eq:DifferenceOfCorrelations.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(\vec{a},\vec{b})-P(\vec{a},\vec{c})=-\int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)[A(\vec{a},\lambda)A(\vec{b},\lambda)-A(\vec{a},\lambda)A(\vec{c},\lambda)].
          %p
            Rearranging this we may write
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:DifferenceOfCorrelations"} (2)
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(\vec{a},\vec{b})-P(\vec{a},\vec{c})=-\int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)A(\vec{a},\lambda)A(\vec{b},\lambda)[A(\vec{b},\lambda)A(\vec{c},\lambda)-1].
          %p
            Given the limitations we have placed on the value of
            = succeed "," do
              %em A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  |P(\vec{a},\vec{b})-P(\vec{a},\vec{c})| \le \int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)[1-A(\vec{b},\lambda)A(\vec{c},\lambda)].
          %p
            But the second term on the right is simply
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\vec{b},\vec{c})
            and thus
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1 + P(\vec{b},\vec{c}) \ge |P(\vec{a},\vec{b})-P(\vec{a},\vec{c})|
          %p which is the original form of Bell’s inequality. Note that this may be written in terms of correlation coefficients,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1 + C(b,c) \ge |C(a,b)-C(a,c)|
          %p
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %em a
            = succeed "," do
              %em b
            %em c
            are now settings on the measurement apparatus.
          %h4#quantum_mechanical_violations Quantum mechanical violations
          %p
            The original derivation of Bell’s inequalities involved the use of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stern-Gerlach+device"} Stern-Gerlach device
            that measures spin along an axis. Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{1}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{2}
            are spins. The result,
            = succeed "," do
              %em A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{1}\cdot\vec{a}
            is then interpreted as being entirely determined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %em B
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{2}\cdot\vec{b}
            %em B
            does not depend on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            and likewise
            %em A
            does not depend on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{b}
          %p For a singlet state (that is a state with total spin of zero), the quantum mechanical expectation value of measurements along two different axes (see the Wigner derivation below for a more intuitive explanation of the physical nature of this) is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle\sigma_{1}\cdot\vec{a},\sigma_{2}\cdot\vec{b}\rangle = - \vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}.
          %p
            In theory this ought to equal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\vec{a},\vec{b})
            but in practice it does not. It is important to remember that we are using
            %em classical
            reasoning throughout our derivations of the various forms of Bell’s inequalities.
          %p
            The setup envisioned here consists of pairs of spin-1/2 particles produced in singlet states that then each pass through separate Stern-Gerlach (SG) devices. Since they are in singlet states, if we measured the first particle of a pair to be aligned with a given axis, say
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
          %p
            In practice we are dealing with
            %em beams
            of particles and thus we can never be absolutely certain that correlated pairs are measured simultaneously and so we ultimately are making statistical predictions. Nevertheless, in a given sample consisting of a large-enough number of randomly distributed spin-1/2 particles, we can be certain that, for example, a definite number are aligned with an axis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            while a definite number are aligned with an axis
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{b}
          %p
            Now take an individual particle and suppose that, for this particle, if we measured
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\cdot\vec{a}
            we would obtain a +1 with certainty (meaning it is aligned with
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{a}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\cdot\vec{b}
            we would obtain a -1 with certainty (meaning it is anti-aligned with
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{b}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vec{a}+,\vec{b}-)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vec{a}+,\vec{b}-)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo −
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vec{a}-,\vec{b}+)
          %h4#locality Locality
          %p For beams of correlated particles measuring along only two axes, we should expect to get a roughly evenly balanced distribution of types as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mtext Particle 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mtext Particle 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo +
                          %mo ,
                          %mover
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↔
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo −
                          %mo ,
                          %mover
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo +
                          %mo ,
                          %mover
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↔
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mo −
